WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.660
55 interventions were tested on 20,000 Americans to raise carbon pricing support, writing a letter to a future person ranked among the strongest approaches. This is Planetterrian Daily, Episode 255. Vegas nerve stimulation and gut microbes come next. Then nasal messenger RNA drops an aged mice, and assigns deep dive on glycine clusters. A pre-registered experiment on the prolific platform, reported by Fizz.org, assigned each participant

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.780
one randomly chose an intervention or a control condition. 108 researchers designed the study so

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:41.620
every person saw only a single treatment which kept the comparisons clean. 11 of those treatments

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:48.180
produced a detectable rise in support or a real-world action such as a donation. Imagining one's own

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.280
life decades ahead, with climate action or without it, also raised donations. Information that

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.640
carbon pricing cuts emissions, or that revenue is recycled into climate protection, showed

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.980
measurable effects as well.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.000
The average effect across the full set of design stayed small, yet it was statistically

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:06.800
significant.

00:01:07.818 --> 00:01:12.718
Stimulation of the vagus nerve changes the makeup of intestinal microbes in ways that lessened

00:01:12.718 --> 00:01:15.958
seizure activity, according to cell host and microbe.

00:01:16.258 --> 00:01:21.098
The pathway runs from nerve signaling through those bacteria and onto brain excitability.

00:01:21.098 --> 00:01:26.378
Altering the microbial community is what lowered seizure activity in the reported experiments.

00:01:26.858 --> 00:01:31.238
The same nerve that carries signals from gut to brain is the starting point of the chain.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:37.425
Wide spread inflammatory signals drive T cells into an exhausted state, a loop described

00:01:37.425 --> 00:01:38.105
in immunity.

00:01:38.725 --> 00:01:43.425
Once those cells are pushed into exhaustion, the process feeds on itself and keeps the

00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:44.205
impairment going.

00:01:44.885 --> 00:01:47.925
Body-wide inflammation is what initiates the negative feedback.

00:01:47.925 --> 00:01:51.925
The result is an immune response that cannot sustain its own attack.

00:01:52.992 --> 00:01:58.052
Angelica Harbauer's group at the Max Planck Institute for Biological Intelligence showed

00:01:58.052 --> 00:02:02.892
that neurons make a mitochondrial quality control protein right beside their mitochondria,

00:02:03.472 --> 00:02:08.372
work covered by Fizz.org. The cell does not wait for that protein to travel the length

00:02:08.372 --> 00:02:13.892
of the axon. The strategy is compared to onsite tracer production in hospitals, where

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:19.492
needed molecule is generated next to the thing it must check. Rapid damage checks become possible

00:02:19.492 --> 00:02:24.872
because synthesis happens locally. Long distance transport is not required for this particular

00:02:24.872 --> 00:02:31.001
quality control step. Nasal messenger RNA drops restore memory performance in aged mice

00:02:31.001 --> 00:02:37.101
and in Alzheimer's model mice, according to Science Daily. The drops stimulate production of a neuron

00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:43.041
protecting protein and they do so without clearing plaques. Treated older mice regained youthful

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:47.821
learning and memory scores. Alzheimer's model mice showed prevented cognitive decline

00:02:47.821 --> 00:02:54.041
rather than a reversal of plaques. The delivery uses technology similar to messenger RNA vaccines,

00:02:54.601 --> 00:03:00.965
only aimed at a protective protein inside neurons. Gau Chishia's group at the Institute of Genetics

00:03:00.965 --> 00:03:06.545
and Developmental Biology published a step-by-step experimental protocol in nature protocols

00:03:06.545 --> 00:03:11.965
cells, as Fizz.org notes, for targeted chromosome manipulation in plant genomes.

00:03:12.365 --> 00:03:17.345
The platform lets researchers rearrange plant chromosomes on purpose rather than waiting

00:03:17.345 --> 00:03:18.805
on rare natural events.

00:03:19.525 --> 00:03:23.805
Guidance in the protocol walks through the workflow from design to the edited chromosome.

00:03:24.825 --> 00:03:29.225
Programmable chromosome editing in plants is now written out as a method others can follow.

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:35.541
Alphalfa at the same Institute of Genetics and Developmental Biology can form root nodules

00:03:35.541 --> 00:03:36.601
without rhizobia.

00:03:37.161 --> 00:03:42.521
A cytokine in nodule inception regulatory module drives that spontaneous nodule formation.

00:03:43.221 --> 00:03:47.741
The plant therefore starts nodules on its own, independent of bacterial partners.

00:03:48.381 --> 00:03:53.081
The work maps an alternative pathway for nodule organogenesis in this forage crop.

00:03:54.243 --> 00:03:59.203
Embarry underwater robots collected specimens of an unfamiliar cell, first seen more than

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:00.103
20 years ago.

00:04:00.103 --> 00:04:05.123
Larry Medine led the formal description of vitreosalpa Gemini, the glass

00:04:05.123 --> 00:04:09.823
salp. The species lives exclusively in deep water. It is the first known

00:04:09.823 --> 00:04:14.788
salp confined to that habitat. University of Nottingham researchers track

00:04:14.788 --> 00:04:20.228
nanoscale transformations inside working catalyst particles. Electron microscopy

00:04:20.228 --> 00:04:23.528
captured structural shifts that appear while the catalyst is actually

00:04:23.528 --> 00:04:28.068
operating. Those dynamic changes had not been watched in this way during real

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:32.968
catalytic work. The observations are intended to guide design of more efficient catalyst

00:04:32.968 --> 00:04:39.208
for cleaner fuels. Natsall, the national survey of sexual attitudes and lifestyles, collected

00:04:39.208 --> 00:04:45.068
data from a large national sample on British sexual behavior since 1990. Results appear

00:04:45.068 --> 00:04:50.848
across five papers in the Lancet. This is the fourth Natsall survey in that series. Sexual

00:04:50.848 --> 00:04:56.821
and reproductive health needs remain substantial, and they vary across age groups. Sultan Kaboos

00:04:56.821 --> 00:05:01.301
University researchers examined gills and digestive tracks of fish from the sea of

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:07.501
Oman near Sohar. They sampled 120 fish and detected microplastics in 95 of them.

00:05:08.061 --> 00:05:13.481
That is nearly 80% of the sample. The measurement supply a baseline for later monitoring along

00:05:13.481 --> 00:05:19.643
that coast. Most people assume that molecular clusters grow steadily more stable as extra units

00:05:19.643 --> 00:05:25.963
join, yet glycine does the opposite and locks in at a single size. Clusters of the amino acid

00:05:25.963 --> 00:05:31.483
reach a sharp peak of stability at precisely six molecules. Each glycine switches from its

00:05:31.483 --> 00:05:37.303
neutral form to a charged zwitterion once that six-member cluster assembles. The charged form

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:42.703
lets neighbors hold one another with stronger electrostatic bonds. That internal rearrangement

00:05:42.703 --> 00:05:48.483
happens only at that specific size. Smaller groupings and larger ones fall apart more readily because

00:05:48.483 --> 00:05:53.703
they never make the same switch. Controlled cluster experiments isolated different numbers of

00:05:53.703 --> 00:05:56.083
lysine units and compared them side-by-side.

00:05:56.583 --> 00:06:01.503
The same structural flip may operate in other small peptides, which would change how chemists

00:06:01.503 --> 00:06:04.723
predict which assemblies persist in solution or on surfaces.

00:06:05.443 --> 00:06:10.283
Knowing the exact size of the switch lets them design more durable molecular scaffolds without

00:06:10.283 --> 00:06:11.983
adding extra stabilizing groups.

00:06:12.994 --> 00:06:18.314
A new chemical route achieved the first total synthesis of the antitussive alkaloid Neo-Tuberos

00:06:18.314 --> 00:06:24.154
morning, after nearly 30 years of attempts, and it clarified how nitrogen geometry contributes

00:06:24.154 --> 00:06:29.474
to oxidative instability. A multi-institutional Japanese team used laser light to map

00:06:29.474 --> 00:06:35.074
how electrons move and accumulate when atomically thin semi-conductors are stacked, a root

00:06:35.074 --> 00:06:41.001
toward lower power electronics. Flowers carved on Benvenudo Shilini's Narcissus connect

00:06:41.001 --> 00:06:46.621
emerging botanical science with the way Renaissance artists competed, and with Cosimo the first

00:06:46.621 --> 00:06:49.221
to Medici's push to strengthen his rule and Florence.

00:06:49.741 --> 00:06:53.961
The plants on the sculpture are rendered with accurate botanical detail, rather than

00:06:53.961 --> 00:06:54.981
as generic ornaments.

00:06:55.641 --> 00:07:00.961
That accuracy served artistic goals, because rival sculptors were being judged on how closely

00:07:00.961 --> 00:07:02.541
they observed the natural world.

00:07:03.081 --> 00:07:08.301
It also served political ones, because Cosimo I could display command of new knowledge as

00:07:08.301 --> 00:07:09.881
part of his authority in the city.

00:07:09.881 --> 00:07:16.261
The analysis traces how a single set of carved blossoms tied science, competition, and

00:07:16.261 --> 00:07:18.581
Medici ambition together in one object.

00:07:19.221 --> 00:07:22.781
Accurate plant representation was doing more than decorating marble.

00:07:23.361 --> 00:07:27.121
It was a public claim about who understood nature and who governed Florence.

00:07:28.168 --> 00:07:33.508
Before we go, chemists can now test whether forcing or blocking the Zwitterian transition

00:07:33.508 --> 00:07:37.028
changes how long glycine clusters last in different solvents.

